--- title
# LOGV Learn

--- body
Course publishing, study progress and paid enrollment within a single learning product.

--- actions
[All products](/en/#products)
:::

::: section topic-1
## Content and study

The content model distinguishes a course from its modules and lessons. Administrative work concerns editing and publishing those materials; the learner's experience concerns viewing content, resuming a course and preserving progress.

Keeping these activities separate matters because the person editing a lesson and the person who completed it have different reasons to access the same content.
:::

::: section topic-2
## Enrollment and access

Paid enrollment adds an asynchronous boundary. A payment may be initiated in one request while confirmation arrives later. The learning application must connect that outcome to a record of who is entitled to access the course.

A single web application can host these functions without treating publishing, student progress and commercial access as one undifferentiated workflow.
:::

::: section topic-3
## Engineering considerations

The important consistency questions concern repeated payment notifications, changes in enrollment status and progress that should survive a new session. Reliable access cannot depend solely on what the client interface displays.

The reviewed repository establishes the existence of these application flows. Comprehensive automated coverage of the purchase-to-access lifecycle was not demonstrated in this review.
:::

